prompt cache: HALOGEN_CACHE_ENTRIES=4 is below the 6 this configuration needs to keep one conversation's worth of entries per slot (1 slot(s) x [1 anchor + 4 leaves + 1 full]). Conversations past that many lose their history to the global LRU and re-prefill in full; raise it, or lower HALOGEN_CACHE_BRANCHES if the memory matters more.
halogen: WARNING 24.0 GiB of RAM is carved out for the iGPU in firmware, and this model reads a 47.7 GiB lookup table through the host file cache on every request. A carve-out that large competes directly with that cache, and the symptom is a server that starts, answers short prompts, and then crawls on a long one with the disk busy and a process in uninterruptible sleep.
  You are already paying for it even if nothing has thrashed yet: the KV pool sizes itself from MemTotal, which the carve-out has made smaller, so the 'kv pool' line above gave up resident conversations to keep the cache whole. Pin the pool yourself and the cache takes the whole loss instead.
  This engine does not need dedicated VRAM: it drives the GPU through GTT and allocates from the same unified memory either way. Set the BIOS UMA or dedicated-graphics-memory option back to Auto or its minimum, which reports about 512 MiB here, and the carved RAM returns to the host.
  Lowering HALOGEN_KV_POOL_POSITIONS or raising HALOGEN_HOST_RESERVE_GIB buys cache back on the host side if the BIOS cannot be changed.
halogen: NOTE this server now holds most of the machine, and 182 contiguous 2 MiB blocks remain. It runs best on a host of its own. If other large processes share this machine they will compete for what is left, and when it runs out BOTH they and this server can stall for minutes at a time at full CPU with no disk activity and no output, which is not a crash and needs no restart. Lowering HALOGEN_KV_POOL_POSITIONS gives some back. HALOGEN_FLASH_PIN_TRUNK=0 gives a great deal back and costs several times the decode speed, so it is a last resort.
